{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-75045",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14247841/assets/0c68391a7a23867f21ee851aa86d389ff4dc186c3aaedde0ccb1fe15b5b4ee5b.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+2$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+2$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14247841/assets/144142b823e7db82eb9b65e6ddc835d75a7fd8d218eab17f9db9019c4b8ab109.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14247841/assets/a2c948117d63fdd60d16ac29b3900238f7c246201bd45df3e3ee940aac0d4ee6.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(0,0)=2 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(1,0)=3 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(0,1)=3 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(1,1)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =2+3+3+4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
